  "articleBody": "When you're looking at fixing a floor transition, it's easy to make a few common blunders that can lead to more problems down the line. First off, a huge mistake is leaving any old adhesive or gunk in place. You've got to get that area super clean for the new adhesive to bond properly. Secondly, don't just pick any transition strip; choosing the wrong profile for your specific floor heights or materials is a recipe for disaster. The strip won't sit right, and you'll end up with gaps or an uneven surface. Another big one is failing to leave enough room for expansion. Flooring materials naturally expand and contract with temperature and humidity, and if you don't account for that, your strips will loosen, warp, or create new gaps. Lastly, if the issue stems from a failing subfloor or a significant height difference, a quick patch might not be enough. If you're dealing with anything more complex than a simple re-glue, or if it's becoming a trip hazard, definitely give a pro a call. They can assess the underlying problem and ensure a lasting fix.",
  "datePublished": "2026-08-09"
}